Watch EndpointSlices. As of Kubernetes 1.33 the Endpoints API is officially deprecated, and the API server returns warnings to anyone who reads or writes Endpoints resources. Newer Service features, such as dual-stack networking and traffic distribution, are only supported through EndpointSlices. For that reason, service proxies, Gateway API implementations and similar controllers have all moved to EndpointSlices. kube-proxy no longer uses Endpoints at all.

The Endpoints controller may also not be running in a given cluster. The plan in KEP-4974 is to change conformance so it no longer requires the Endpoints (and EndpointSlice mirroring) controllers, and to make disabling them a supported configuration.

When consuming EndpointSlices, note that a Service can have several of them, not exactly one object named after the Service. You list them with the label `kubernetes.io/service-name=<service>`. Multiple slices appear in these cases:
- separate slices for IPv4 and IPv6 in dual-stack Services;
- separate slices for endpoints targeting different ports (for example during a port-change rollout);
- splitting when a Service has more than 100 endpoints.
